from the old medieval world. Let's take a

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.490
look.
In the medieval world, there were two things.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:41.400
Body and Soul. The soul was the knot of the
universe, because it integrated belief in

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:48.240
God, cosmology, life on Earth, psychology,
the difference in life and death. All those

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:54.380
things. That started to crack with the scientific
revolution. The world started to change. Historian

00:04:54.380 --> 00:05:00.419
and psychiatrist George Makari's new book
is called Soul Machine: The Invention of the

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:08.220
Modern Mind. The third entity, the mind, started
to emerge as a natural alternative for the

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:15.250
place for inner life. In the 1500's, notions
of a secular mind were already lurking. Johann

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:20.160
Weyer of Holland dared to suggest to the Church
Fathers that not all witches should be burned

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.780
at the stake, because some of them might not
have made covenant with the devil ,but just

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.000
be suffering from a disease that crippled
their reasoning. In the 1600s, Oxford Medical

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.460
Doctor, Thomas Willis, began dissecting and
mapping the brain, launching modern neuroscience

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.800
in the West. And his most famous student,
philosopher John Locke, developed a science-

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.729
based theory of mind and of self that said
they were based partly on imperfect, subjective

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:54.730
impressions. The mind was highly scandalous,
very dangerous as an idea, and was one that

00:05:54.730 --> 00:06:01.340
very much had a lot of political and scientific,
medical and theological implications that

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:07.500
made it quite explosive. Why? It challenged
absolutism, both in terms of political power

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:13.560
and in terms of clerical power. The mind was
fallible. It could not make absolute claims

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:19.699
about truth. It demanded, therefore, tolerance.
Because if I can't know the absolute truth,

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:24.710
I should tolerate you and you should tolerate
me. It allowed for secular ethics like life,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:31.070
liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.
The idea of a center of reason and will that

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:37.141
was not immortal caught on and, says Makari,
helped change the way the West was run. It's

00:06:37.141 --> 00:06:41.930
the centerpiece of the emergence of secular
culture. But there was a problem. Could you

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:47.200
locate this new sort of mind in the brain?
Well the brain is easy to define. I mean,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.139
you could make the cut where you want, but
the brain is an empirical object. The problem

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:59.819
is interior experience and mind is not like
any other object in the natural world. Because

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:08.120
it's not available to the external observer
and always available to us. Today, we have

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.050
