00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:09.129
kind of long sausage-y things on the side.
Oh, yes, yes, right. So, to go back for a

00:26:09.129 --> 00:26:15.110
moment to the slippery concept of consciousness
that made you sit up. I've heard some people

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:19.940
say that consciousness is basically "awareness"
whatever- we're using words to try to get

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:26.690
into this concept her. But Dr. Prinz, what's
the difference between awareness and attention,

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:30.500
for example?
Well I think they're actually the same. I

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:35.129
mean one of the things that we've- I think
been able to do with the science of consciousness

00:26:35.129 --> 00:26:41.330
is start to relate this very ethereal concept,
consciousness, awareness, experience to things

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:46.480
that we understand much better. How does consciousness
relate to memory? How does it relate to attention?

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:50.450
Those are questions that we can ask scientifically
and even- even if we never get a full handle

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:55.080
on how a physical thing like the brain can
give rise to experience, we can understand

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:59.740
what processes in the brain are taking place
when we are conscious. And doing that, we

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:03.490
can learn a lot of things about consciousness
that are very important. So suppose we're

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:09.940
using Joy's new technology on somebody who's
watching a magic show and there's this sleight

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:15.759
of hand. So a very good magician might ,you
know, pull- pull a card out of thin air. And

00:27:15.759 --> 00:27:21.420
how do they do this? Well of course, the card
was somewhere and if they had a camera trained

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:26.440
on the sleeve or wherever it was located,
it would be very easy to consciously experience

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:30.909
its original location. But a good magician
is going to distract. A good magician might

00:27:30.909 --> 00:27:37.539
use hand gestures or even voice to get the
viewer to miss out on the trick. I think conversation

00:27:37.539 --> 00:27:41.380
is like that in general. When we're having
a conversation, we're directing each other's

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:45.830
attention and there are many things we might
be attending to. Might be attending to the

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:51.119
emotional expression of our conversation partner
we might be attending to to ourselves. How

00:27:51.119 --> 00:27:55.289
do we sound? How are we self presenting? We
might be attending to social hierarchy. Is

00:27:55.289 --> 00:28:00.739
this somebody who has a higher status?
Neuroscientists, psychologists studying conversation

00:28:00.739 --> 00:28:06.340
very rarely study attention. They very rarely
study power. They very rarely study social

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:12.929
dynamics. They rarely study things like compassion
or aggression or impatience. So conversation

00:28:12.929 --> 00:28:18.769
isn't just a string of words, it's a it's
a kind of a multiple channels of information

00:28:18.769 --> 00:28:22.260
that are directing what people notice and
what they tend to. The conscious experience

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:27.260
you have, what you notice, the content you
take away, is not just enumerated in the words

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:30.519
that are presented to you, but in all of these
other cues that are telling you what the meaning

00:28:30.519 --> 00:28:34.049
is.
So- and consciousness is...?

00:28:34.049 --> 00:28:39.190
So attention is, I think, something that allows
information to flow in the nervous system.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.879
When you're having a perceptual encounter
with the world, a lot of what gets in gets

00:28:42.879 --> 00:28:46.399
in subliminally. We're totally unaware of
it. You can measure this you can show that

00:28:46.399 --> 00:28:51.269
if you present something very quickly say
for 17 milliseconds, followed by a mask- people

00:28:51.269 --> 00:28:56.679
have no capacity to experience it whatsoever.
As a journalist, I noticed-I asked you- so

00:28:56.679 --> 00:29:00.740
consciousness is? And you very suddenly said
attention. So attention is this?

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:06.590
So attention is the difference between something
getting stuck in your perceptual systems and

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:11.789
sort of hanging out there indefinitely and
passing forward to other parts of the nervous

00:29:11.789 --> 00:29:15.799
system where you can think about it, use it
in reasoning, use it in deliberation. Consciousness

00:29:15.799 --> 00:29:21.139
is attention and attention is this doorway
that opens that allows perception to access

00:29:21.139 --> 00:29:23.749
the rest of
Consciousness is attention and this all sounds

00:29:23.749 --> 00:29:25.429
very logical-
Can I disagree?

00:29:25.429 --> 00:29:31.409
Yes, yes please.
I mean, so I would say attention is maybe

00:29:31.409 --> 00:29:36.779
necessary for consciousness. But, you know,
I don't get the heebie jeebies when people

00:29:36.779 --> 00:29:48.429
talk about attention. That's a good mainstream
cognitive neuroscience concept. The- but consciousness

00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:56.100
means in these kinds of discussions, that
subjective sense of what something feels like.

00:29:56.100 --> 00:30:00.260
How do we know-I
know that I'm paying attention? Is there a

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:05.860
subject- usually subjective?
So you know Ned Block, a colleague of yours.

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:17.269
Yes at NYU philosophy, distinguishes between
consciousness of A for access. Or you could

00:30:17.269 --> 00:30:23.529
say attention too, that is consciousness in
the sense that we can access some information,

00:30:23.529 --> 00:30:29.309
we can access that somebody looked surprised
by what we've just said, or you know whatever,

00:30:29.309 --> 00:30:38.679
and consciousness of P for phenomenal or phenomenology.
Right? That kind of feeling of what it's like

00:30:38.679 --> 00:30:47.100
to be experiencing that. And I think whenever
we're talking about subjective mental states,

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:55.389
phenomenological mental states, I think that's-
that's when I do jump when people hit me with

00:30:55.389 --> 00:30:58.299
"consciousness." you know, when I'm not expecting
it

00:30:58.299 --> 00:31:03.649
It's spooky.
It is spooky. I think we are profoundly ignorant

00:31:03.649 --> 00:31:08.440
and probably always will be about how you
get that subjectivity out of it.

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:11.749
I've been thinking about this problem before,
I felt a great relief when I heard there was

00:31:11.749 --> 00:31:16.289
a philosopher named Thomas Nagel who solves
the problem. He said consciousness is simply

00:31:16.289 --> 00:31:21.570
one of the irreducible of the universe, is
just there like gravity and mass and various

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:26.299
creatures tap into it and reconstruct it in
various ways. So

00:31:26.299 --> 00:31:32.299
This to me, is a few-Tom Nagel was a teacher
of mine. He brought me to this subject and

00:31:32.299 --> 00:31:37.080
I'm very much in his debt. Ned is a colleague,
Ned Block and we've talked together and work

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:41.500
together on various things. I think there's
a way in which we get ourselves into this

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:47.950
air of mystery. It's needless. And it creates
problems that aren't really there. When people

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:51.549
used to talk about life, they were absolutely
mystified how life could figure in the physical

00:31:51.549 --> 00:31:58.019
world. And when things like the structure
of DNA were discovered, it was a major intellectual

00:31:58.019 --> 00:32:02.259
breakthrough because suddenly this thing that
seemed absolutely mystified, absolutely impossible

00:32:02.259 --> 00:32:07.859
to explain, was understood in physical terms.
Descartes thought the big mystery of the mind

00:32:07.859 --> 00:32:12.730
was not conscious experience but reason. Descartes,
maybe one of the greatest minds in the history

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:17.730
of Western thought, could not figure out how
reason could be physically explained. Then

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:23.350
computers came along and we have a program.
That in a very simple way, is doing complex

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:27.740
derivations of mathematical proofs. That's
reasoning. So I think we're going to get to

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:32.970
a point with consciousness where once we found
out what are the psychological processes and

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:36.779
the neurobiological processes that take place
that make the difference between seeing something

00:32:36.779 --> 00:32:43.059
and not. Then we're going to be in a position
to say that some of this sort of wallowing

00:32:43.059 --> 00:32:48.080
in mystery was really unnecessary. And other
very exciting questions can be answered.

00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:52.679
Well, I know that you're nodding and so maybe
