00:48:30.390 --> 00:48:35.240
my collaborators and I assume something like
this was right and we wondered is this really

00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:40.450
how ordinary people think? About identity.
And we started to test it. We started to test

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:45.140
it in various ways, but typically using questionnaires.
We ask people about a bunch of traits.

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:49.170
A bunch of?
A bunch of traits- your memories, your cognitive

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:54.300
capacities, your personality, your vocations
in life, your occupation, where you live

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:57.990
And you're not using the word "identity" as
synonymous with self.

00:48:57.990 --> 00:49:04.140
We -our interest is in the question, "What
makes somebody the same self? What changes

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:07.850
take place? All of us are changing all the
time. Human beings are radically dynamic.

00:49:07.850 --> 00:49:14.140
We are the most capable of learning of any
creature we know and that means we're constantly

00:49:14.140 --> 00:49:17.950
just sitting here- every time you hear a sentence,
your brain has been rewired, something is

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:22.910
getting stored, a new trace is there, but
you feel like you're the same person. So part

00:49:22.910 --> 00:49:26.780
of understanding what the self is, is understanding
what remains constant such that you can say,

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:31.030
"I'm still me this morning. I'm still the
same person I was this morning. Even though

00:49:31.030 --> 00:49:35.380
