Yeah let's let's have a let's have a look.
So. Now what you're going to do now is you're

00:58:00.942 --> 00:58:05.180
going to vote. You're going to vote by show
of hands.

00:58:05.180 --> 00:58:10.390
So this person isn't wearing any political
buttons or any- any kind of paraphernalia

00:58:10.390 --> 00:58:15.840
that would tell you. How many of you think
this is a Republican raise your hands. How

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:27.190
many of you think this is a Democrat? So why?
You know that's really weird.

00:58:27.190 --> 00:58:33.660
Let's look at another example. OK. So in some
cases maybe- maybe. Let's see how this one

00:58:33.660 --> 00:58:40.960
goes. How many think this is a Republican?
How many say Democrat? OK. So still big, big,

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:48.000
big majority. Next one. OK.
So what do we have here. How many you say

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:54.880
Republican in this case? OK the house goes
up. And Democrat anyone? So yes, I mean, obviously

00:58:54.880 --> 00:58:59.410
we know we all know counter-examples. We all
know people who spent their- their- put their

00:58:59.410 --> 00:59:03.990
lives on the line for this country working
for military causes, even though they vote

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:08.480
for the Democratic Party, that's not unheard
of. But the fact that we can signal this in

00:59:08.480 --> 00:59:12.660
some way, is an indication that- the fact
we have such consensus on these opinions shows

00:59:12.660 --> 00:59:16.700
how important this is in social decision making.
Let's look at another case.

00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:24.240
OK how many say Republican? How can say Democrat?
Wow. So overwhelming. It's just that she's

00:59:24.240 --> 00:59:29.850
wearing black. Right? We're trying to figure
out what drives these decisions. Let's look

00:59:29.850 --> 00:59:35.230
at one more example and you'll get a kind
of complete. For this. OK how many say Republican

00:59:35.230 --> 00:59:40.620
here? OK so here we got the majority. Anyone
want to say Democrat here? A few. And I think

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:50.110
there's one more. OK so how many say Republican?
How many say Democrat? So very very- for those

00:59:50.110 --> 00:59:53.890
of you who have not been able to see the hands
go up- we're probably- if we measure this

00:59:53.890 --> 00:59:58.930
room, we'd probably be in the 90s percentile
in terms of agreement on which one is a Republican

00:59:58.930 --> 01:00:02.430
and which one is a Democrat. Sometimes it's
ethnicity, sometimes it's occupation, like

01:00:02.430 --> 01:00:06.430
the military commitment. But what about the
beard? What about the black hat? The fact

01:00:06.430 --> 01:00:10.750
that randomly chosen people on the street
are conveying to others what party they belong

01:00:10.750 --> 01:00:14.780
to, I think is one of the most revealing things
I've encountered in my investigations about

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:33.820
the self. How many think I'm a Republican?
This raises a lot of questions to me. Why

01:00:33.820 --> 01:00:40.070
are you applauding?
What are you applauding for?

01:00:40.070 --> 01:00:43.190
Why were you so amused that you laughed at
each one of his questions? I mean this is

01:00:43.190 --> 01:00:47.500
a very serious question. What was so amusing
about this. I've got other questions such

01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:53.330
as if you showed these pictures to somebody
in a different time in a different century.

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:56.040
Very important.
Even if they didn't have- they might have

01:00:56.040 --> 01:01:00.890
looked conservative for some people, whatever
that means. So in other words, you're also

01:01:00.890 --> 01:01:05.310
showing us if this empirical data is building
up some of the understandings of some of the

01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:11.640
ugly sides of human nature or parts of it,
such as stereotypes and judgmental-I mean

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:19.860
this is talking about the basis of our judgment.
Martha? Once again, you're nodding a lot.

01:01:19.860 --> 01:01:25.540
That's my striatum, right? I have to reprogram
my striatum. Get out of that habit.

01:01:25.540 --> 01:01:34.490
No but I mean- you also definitely- you were.
Oh since you are probing, I'll say that I

01:01:34.490 --> 01:01:38.390
thought the most-I think there is- what's
in one of the interesting things, if I may,

01:01:38.390 --> 01:01:42.350
is I'm outside of my neuroscience area of
expertise here, was that I think people were

01:01:42.350 --> 01:01:47.300
laughing because we all recognize it's silly
to reach these conclusions, at least in part.

01:01:47.300 --> 01:01:51.690
But I actually think that Jesse's last comment
was the most interesting one. The point is

01:01:51.690 --> 01:01:56.070
not that we have strong opinions about this.
We know that people use stereotypes and all

01:01:56.070 --> 01:02:01.300
kinds of ways. I think the point is that all
of us use- take advantage of those cues in

01:02:01.300 --> 01:02:07.140
our choices every day. So it's a social projection
of that, which I thought was actually directly

01:02:07.140 --> 01:02:13.520
related to what Joy was saying about how we
communicate nonverbally and verbally and in

01:02:13.520 --> 01:02:19.690
that way because we're soliciting -are- people
like us to know- we're signaling to them.

01:02:19.690 --> 01:02:23.490
So you three brain scientists who actually
look at the gray matter in the purple and

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:31.260
green and dark matters and white matters.
Find something to talk about with this philosopher

01:02:31.260 --> 01:02:36.500
that may be able to match your scientific
data from your brain labs with the type of

01:02:36.500 --> 01:02:41.190
data that he's turning up from these. Could
you explain the connection there?

01:02:41.190 --> 01:02:46.570
Let me just say little bit that. I think that
one of the things that fascinates me the most

01:02:46.570 --> 01:02:52.850
about what you're talking about, is what it
tells us about our brains and how actively

01:02:52.850 --> 01:03:02.110
we interrogate and seek information about
others and how we relate that to ourselves.

01:03:02.110 --> 01:03:09.300
It's as if in that seeking process, we become
unified in some way with who with the other

01:03:09.300 --> 01:03:14.810
and I think that's the next stage of neuroscience
is going to tell us a lot more about those

01:03:14.810 --> 01:03:21.900
very fundamental mechanisms. And one of my
goals in neuroscience is to move this most

01:03:21.900 --> 01:03:31.660
fascinating idea from the hands of philosophers
and put it- well maybe- maybe empirical philosophers

01:03:31.660 --> 01:03:36.400
are different, and but put it in the hands
of neuroscience so that we can understand

01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:43.100
the neural machinery, the neural mechanisms
that drive this connection that we have with

01:03:43.100 --> 01:03:47.610
other people and how it is we interrogate
other people to understand who they are.

01:03:47.610 --> 01:03:54.490
If I can if I can quickly come to the defense.
Well I mean I really look forward to that

01:03:54.490 --> 01:03:58.470
day and I think the stuff you're doing with
really the kinds of methods that can study

01:03:58.470 --> 01:04:03.740
social interaction with brain imaging are
extremely exciting. One of the problems that

01:04:03.740 --> 01:04:08.380
I've had with neuroscience, as a field, given
its current technologies, is it really misses

01:04:08.380 --> 01:04:14.710
out on the social. We have in this culture,
biophilia. We think that if something is biologically

01:04:14.710 --> 01:04:20.650
explained, it's real and if it's cultural
or about ideas or even about the social, it's

01:04:20.650 --> 01:04:26.030
somehow less real. So we have for instance,
if you look at- the National Institute of

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:31.430
Health funds studies on addiction, like alcoholism.
Overwhelmingly, that money goes to studies

01:04:31.430 --> 01:04:35.430
looking at genes and brain circuits, right?
It's looking at those factors. But we know

01:04:35.430 --> 01:04:40.710
that sociogenic factors, factors about where
you live, factors about poverty, which Martha

01:04:40.710 --> 01:04:45.720
studied. All of those things are at least
maybe more important studies of genetic relatedness

01:04:45.720 --> 01:04:49.850
show that if you're an alcoholic, there there's
about a 15 percent chance that your offspring

01:04:49.850 --> 01:04:54.310
will be alcoholics. That means the overwhelming
majority of them are not. Those studies, if

01:04:54.310 --> 01:04:59.320
they're done with adoption, the effects are
tiny, often go away, or very- very inconsistent

01:04:59.320 --> 01:05:04.610
across studies. If they're done cross-culturally,
the numbers begin to look ridiculous. So America

01:05:04.610 --> 01:05:09.560
has about a 5 percent alcoholism rate. Russia
is about 16 percent. Islamic countries are

01:05:09.560 --> 01:05:16.460
mostly under 1 percent. So we can have a 16
times increase in whether you become an alcoholic

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:20.110
if you're born in Russia as opposed to in
Iran.

01:05:20.110 --> 01:05:23.680
And you're looking forward to the time when
the neurological brain scientists can- can

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:27.430
connect why these things happen.
That's the future I'm excited about, bringing

01:05:27.430 --> 01:05:32.530
culture into the brain, showing how the circuits
of the brain are playing a role in taking

01:05:32.530 --> 01:05:36.980
that cultural information and driving behavior
with them.

01:05:36.980 --> 01:05:42.460
This is not what the neuroscientists, or one
of them is supposed to say, but I'll say it

01:05:42.460 --> 01:05:47.440
anyway. Which is yes, I would like very much
for us to understand this at a neural level,

01:05:47.440 --> 01:05:53.490
and many of us would, but I think maybe I'd
kind of back up Jesse's point and say it even

01:05:53.490 --> 01:06:00.720
more strongly, which is there are many societal
questions about behavior that we know that

01:06:00.720 --> 01:06:04.991
that behavior emerges from the brain, because
it's not coming from anywhere else. But when

01:06:04.991 --> 01:06:09.370
understanding the brain doesn't necessarily
help us treat that behavior. And there's a

01:06:09.370 --> 01:06:13.440
lot of other information that's necessary
and so I argue that for things like understanding

01:06:13.440 --> 01:06:18.590
"is there a self? What is a self? What does
this have to do with how we socialize.?" And

01:06:18.590 --> 01:06:24.590
any questions at that level, the complex questions
about what it means to human. We're never

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:31.230
going to understand them only in terms of
neurons. Maybe not never. Maybe one day. But

01:06:31.230 --> 01:06:37.850
when that day gets closer, it will be the
psychologists and the philosophers who have

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:42.090
set us up to start thinking about those questions
in intelligent ways. Just saying that something

01:06:42.090 --> 01:06:46.230
happens in the brain in of itself doesn't
tell us how it happens.

01:06:46.230 --> 01:06:51.080
It's neutral it doesn't have morality connected
to it. And also, the more you guys have talked

01:06:51.080 --> 01:06:55.250
about learning about the brain, the more potential
dangers of hacking into the brain, of misusing

01:06:55.250 --> 01:06:59.951
this information. The old human problem, learning
things that, I mean, you could learn how to

01:06:59.951 --> 01:07:05.810
control minds or you could do DNA.
So Martha's done some thought in depth about

01:07:05.810 --> 01:07:09.260
these issues of sort of the ethics of neuroscience.
Ethics.

01:07:09.260 --> 01:07:11.590
That's right.
Yeah, well.

01:07:11.590 --> 01:07:17.310
How do you apply them?
You know to understand a system, to thoroughly

01:07:17.310 --> 01:07:22.960
understand a system is to be able to control
it. Right? To predict it and even potentially

01:07:22.960 --> 01:07:30.610
to control it. And, you know, you can you
can rest easy tonight knowing that we neuroscientists

01:07:30.610 --> 01:07:39.160
are still pretty clueless about how this incredibly
complex system works, you know. But, we are

01:07:39.160 --> 01:07:45.460
learning something. I mean, I think the things
that you've heard from Joy and Daphna, you

01:07:45.460 --> 01:07:51.330
know, show that neuroscience is beginning
to get a handle on some of these things. You

01:07:51.330 --> 01:07:58.550
know, there's a lot of ways to manipulate
brain function. Drugs is sort of the time

01:07:58.550 --> 01:08:07.470
honored way you sort of tweak, you know, the
neurochemistry. But people are now using brain

01:08:07.470 --> 01:08:18.150
stimulation to do that. Even noninvasive brain
stimulation, which sounds almost like an oxymoron,

01:08:18.150 --> 01:08:23.749
right? Like how can you stimulate the brain
but not open up the body? Except that you

01:08:23.749 --> 01:08:29.549
can do that with electrical fields and magnetic
fields. And there's a lot of research now

01:08:29.549 --> 01:08:38.989
and a lot of actual just people doing it.
You can go online and buy devices that will

01:08:38.989 --> 01:08:46.329
electrically stimulate your brain from outside
of your head. And so that's, you know,- now

01:08:46.329 --> 01:08:51.259
that's people doing it for themselves. But
you could imagine going back to your judges

01:08:51.259 --> 01:08:59.659
sentencing people. You know, you could imagine
a sort of well-meaning policy saying, "We're

01:08:59.659 --> 01:09:04.449
not going to just lock you up. We're going
to actually try to rehabilitate you." We just

01:09:04.449 --> 01:09:11.429
need to sort of, you know, regulate your,
you know, left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.

01:09:11.429 --> 01:09:15.060
And- so we're going to just, you know, put
an electrode there.

01:09:15.060 --> 01:09:18.799
Indeed, and sometimes it's good- people who
buy full spectrum light to look at it in the

01:09:18.799 --> 01:09:24.779
morning to beat the winter depression that-
there's all kinds of good benefits here. But,

01:09:24.779 --> 01:09:26.369
Yes.
It could be abused.

01:09:26.369 --> 01:09:30.339
Yeah. I mean you have to ask like who has
the right to, you know, who's brain is it

01:09:30.339 --> 01:09:33.849
anyway? Kind of like who has the right to-
And now we're going to get back to self here,

01:09:33.849 --> 01:09:38.239
but this is already reminding me of that-
this is a beautiful peaceful conversation

01:09:38.239 --> 01:09:41.589
about something that is not always so peaceful,
I gather in the academic world. What is it

01:09:41.589 --> 01:09:47.779
called? What is it? Neuro-Skepticism? I've-
I've shown the books of some neuroscientists

01:09:47.779 --> 01:09:51.589
to some psychologists who are very thoughtful
people who say, well this person knows enough

01:09:51.589 --> 01:09:55.600
about the brain to be dangerous and maybe
someday. But these neurologists think they

01:09:55.600 --> 01:10:00.369
can control things and they can't. You all
are nodding. You're aware that this these

01:10:00.369 --> 01:10:05.520
fights are going on, right?
Indeed. In fact I think that the term is called

01:10:05.520 --> 01:10:07.469
NeuroBollocks.
NeuroBollocks?

01:10:07.469 --> 01:10:15.269
NeuroBollocks is a very popular term that
is in a way a way of trashing neuroscience

01:10:15.269 --> 01:10:21.539
and the notion that we can learn about ourselves.
I could see how a psychiatrist and shrinks

01:10:21.539 --> 01:10:24.900
would be threatened by you guys, because they've-
they've figured out they have to just talk

01:10:24.900 --> 01:10:31.920
to a patient about some event in the past
and get them to just talk. And they fear that

01:10:31.920 --> 01:10:34.652
you all are going to come in and say no, no.
But I'm not representing it correctly.

01:10:34.652 --> 01:10:45.610
I don't think it's fair to reduce it to insecurity
or threat. I think there's sometimes as a

01:10:45.610 --> 01:10:50.360
society, we get so carried away and how exciting
this all is and it's potential down the road

01:10:50.360 --> 01:10:55.869
that there is a feeling that we're already
there. When in truth, it's a very young field,

01:10:55.869 --> 01:11:02.190
understanding complex psychological mental
phenomena is really it's early stages. Right,

01:11:02.190 --> 01:11:05.179
right that's where we started by saying- not
long ago we thought some of this was in the

01:11:05.179 --> 01:11:08.389
heart. So we know it's in the brain and we
know where in the brain. Between that and

01:11:08.389 --> 01:11:13.670
understanding the how of how these things
happen, there's a long way and I think a lot

01:11:13.670 --> 01:11:17.340
of the Neuro-Skepticism, both outside but
also within the field, is really a healthy

01:11:17.340 --> 01:11:22.750
sign of a growing field. Sort of like in its
adolescence, maybe, rather than its infancy,

01:11:22.750 --> 01:11:27.110
where there's a lot of rebellion. Which I
think it's healthy for advancing the science.

01:11:27.110 --> 01:11:32.260
Right. You're reminding me of an ancient-
was it the Egyptians or I forget who- used

01:11:32.260 --> 01:11:36.510
to think that thought and reason happened
not here, but in the liver and that the soul

01:11:36.510 --> 01:11:40.301
was based in the liver. They hadn't quite
figured it out yet. You all of you got a different

01:11:40.301 --> 01:11:43.860
idea about it, but perhaps you're connecting
some of these things. Because the conversation

01:11:43.860 --> 01:11:47.909
we're having in the greenroom about next year
was about how actually it's all the little

01:11:47.909 --> 01:11:54.809
microbes inside of us that are really controlling
us, using us just to walk them around. So

01:11:54.809 --> 01:11:59.790
we have to study the minds and the brains
of the neuro- of the microbes. But that's

01:11:59.790 --> 01:12:05.079
it. That's next year. I mean.
I just want to come on the point that the

01:12:05.079 --> 01:12:10.349
anxiety about neuroscience is connected to
brain control. I think we also need to remember

01:12:10.349 --> 01:12:15.981
that behavior is more typically, and quite
powerfully, controlled by social forces. So

01:12:15.981 --> 01:12:21.320
you know, many of my family members were killed
in the Holocaust. Had they been of a different

01:12:21.320 --> 01:12:25.090
creed, they would have been the perpetrators,
not the victims. And I think we all need to

01:12:25.090 --> 01:12:29.570
remember- so you are guessed my political
orientation, maybe not correctly maybe correctly,

01:12:29.570 --> 01:12:35.420
I won't say. But I will say I was born and
raised in New York City and had I been born

01:12:35.420 --> 01:12:40.170
and raised upstate or in the Midwest or in
the South where I've also lived, I might vote

01:12:40.170 --> 01:12:46.249
for the other party. And so it's very important
to remember that human behavior is dramatically

01:12:46.249 --> 01:12:50.829
affected by things without direct biological
intervention. Right now we're so far away

01:12:50.829 --> 01:12:55.210
technologically from changing behavior by
altering brain circuits that we should be

01:12:55.210 --> 01:12:58.640
much more worried about the social forces
than the biological.

01:12:58.640 --> 01:13:03.820
And you raise a great hope here, which is
that if general acceptance among the world's

01:13:03.820 --> 01:13:08.550
scientists, and philosophers, and economists,
and psychologists of some of these basic patterns

01:13:08.550 --> 01:13:12.690
that are true of all humans, then we see horrendous
behavior that could lead to great damage,

01:13:12.690 --> 01:13:19.420
such as storm troopers marching in perfect
unison. We could possibly, on the new web

01:13:19.420 --> 01:13:24.050
world, this new global brain or global mind,
if we can talk about it in those terms. Spread

01:13:24.050 --> 01:13:27.760
the knowledge that, look folks, this is a
known disease. Let's all get together and

01:13:27.760 --> 01:13:33.190
be open about it and stop it before it happens.
That's very optimistic. But why not, right?

01:13:33.190 --> 01:13:38.519
That's not impossible. A reasonable hope.
I think though-again, the biophilia is at

01:13:38.519 --> 01:13:42.309
work here, because when we start to think
about the bad things in the world as a disease

01:13:42.309 --> 01:13:47.610
on the model, it's some sort of biological
anomaly, we focus on interventions that are

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:53.179
at that level. But most of it- so take violence.
There's a lot of work on criminal genes, or

01:13:53.179 --> 01:13:58.159
people born bad, and maybe there is some variation
in tendency toward aggressive behavior that

01:13:58.159 --> 01:14:02.900
has a base in biology. But the effect size
of social causes, like poverty, which Martha

01:14:02.900 --> 01:14:07.119
has worked on- and I want to say this is that
an extraordinary panel, because all of the

01:14:07.119 --> 01:14:11.429
neuroscientist on this panel study things,
like anorexia nervosa or poverty, that are

01:14:11.429 --> 01:14:16.329
social factors that show that the brain is
not fixed but is constantly changing and adapting

01:14:16.329 --> 01:14:20.820
to the social setting. If you want to change
things like the violence that exists in the

01:14:20.820 --> 01:14:25.760
world, we need to not be thinking about the
cure at the biological level, but at the biosocial.

01:14:25.760 --> 01:14:29.909
And the scientists- and all of you seem to
be telling us more and more that you keep

01:14:29.909 --> 01:14:34.300
discovering that the brain is much more flexible
than anybody imagined. There was a headline

01:14:34.300 --> 01:14:37.469
recently that said that they've discovered
that there is one organ in the body that has

01:14:37.469 --> 01:14:44.449
a different DNA in it- in the same organ you
have a lot of different DNA in the cells.

01:14:44.449 --> 01:14:50.300
The brain. So that your DNA is not-it is not
the DNA in every brain cell but- in other

01:14:50.300 --> 01:14:54.510
words, there is there's all kinds of ways
evolution has taught our bodies apparently

01:14:54.510 --> 01:14:59.929
to take advantage of as much flexibility as
possible to adapt to what's needed. So to

01:14:59.929 --> 01:15:05.949
get back to our central promise, to figure
out what about the self and- and what we think

01:15:05.949 --> 01:15:12.869
about the self, and consciousness, we have
a quote from Thomas Huxley, who is a biologist

01:15:12.869 --> 01:15:19.550
who, if I'm not mistaken, is the fellow who
advertised how interesting Darwin was. "How

01:15:19.550 --> 01:15:25.420
it is that anything so remarkable as a state
of consciousness comes about by irritating

01:15:25.420 --> 01:15:31.060
nervous tissue is just as unaccountable as
the appearance of the Genie when Aladdin rubbed

01:15:31.060 --> 01:15:37.550
his lamp." Do you three brain scientists think
that still pertains? It still is unaccountable

01:15:37.550 --> 01:15:41.730
are you beginning to account for it?
Well I don't think I'm going to shift to studying

01:15:41.730 --> 01:15:47.039
lamps.
For the state of consciousness. I guess the

01:15:47.039 --> 01:15:51.499
question would be for him- what did he think
he meant by state of consciousness?

01:15:51.499 --> 01:15:59.030
I mean I think this gets us back again to
this question of subjectivity versus objectivity,

01:15:59.030 --> 01:16:06.289
which I know sounds like dialogue from an
old Woody Allen film and Diane Keaton says,

01:16:06.289 --> 01:16:18.389
"Yes, but subjectivity of objectivity." You
know. If. If what he means by state of consciousness,

